## ACF strategy

Use ACF for structured content and flexible CMS control.

Required approach:

- Prefer `get_field()` / `get_sub_field()` and explicit escaping.
- Guard ACF calls so the theme does not fatal when ACF is inactive.
- Use Local JSON and commit `acf-json/`.
- Keep field names stable.
- Keep flexible content layouts controlled.
- Use image fields as attachment IDs where practical.
- Render images with `wp_get_attachment_image()`.

## Flexible content is the default for page sections

Use ACF Flexible Content as the default for any page that has editable sections.

Rules:

- Every page template that has more than one content section must use a `page_sections` flexible content field.
- Each section/component is a layout inside that flexible content field.
- Do not use simple top-level ACF fields for page sections that will appear alongside other sections.
- Simple top-level fields are only acceptable for truly isolated single-field data such as a page subtitle or a single override value.
- When adding a new section/component to any page, add it as a new layout in the flexible content field group — not as a new standalone field group.
- The homepage (`front-page.php`) uses `page_sections` flexible content.
- Interior pages (`page.php`, campaign pages, brand pages) should also use `page_sections` flexible content.
- The loop pattern for flexible content is:

```php
if ( function_exists( 'have_rows' ) && have_rows( 'page_sections' ) ) :
    while ( have_rows( 'page_sections' ) ) :
        the_row();

        if ( is_row_layout( 'layout_name' ) ) :
            get_template_part( 'template-parts/components/component-name', null, array(
                'field_one' => get_sub_field( 'field_one' ),
                'field_two' => get_sub_field( 'field_two' ),
            ) );
        endif;

    endwhile;
endif;
```

Recommended field groups:

```text
Options - Brand Settings
Options - Header / Footer
Page - Flexible Sections
Product - Catalog Details
Advice - Article Details
FAQ - Details
Partner - Details
Promotion - Details
```

## Plain CSS direction

Use plain CSS only.

Rules:

- No Tailwind.
- No Bootstrap.
- No Sass unless explicitly requested.
- No CSS-in-JS.
- Use CSS custom properties for design tokens.
- Use component class names.
- Keep specificity low.
- Mobile-first.
- Use responsive images and fluid type/spacing.
- Match PHYTO reference visuals through clean component CSS, not a framework.

Suggested design-token pattern:

```css
:root {
	--brand-color-primary: #111;
	--brand-color-secondary: #f6f1eb;
	--brand-color-accent: #7a5b43;
	--brand-color-text: #111;
	--brand-color-muted: #666;
	--brand-font-base: system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
}
```

## No front-end auth

Do not create:

- login templates
- register templates
- account templates
- profile pages
- member-only content
- wishlist
- order history
- saved addresses
- customer dashboard

If a newsletter form exists, it is only an email marketing signup, not account registration.

If a contact form exists, it is only a form submission, not user creation.

## Tracking, GDPR, cookies, and forms

The broader scope includes:

- Google Tag Manager.
- Meta Pixel / Facebook Pixel.
- Newsletter/email marketing integration.
- GDPR/cookie consent.
- reCAPTCHA or other form protection where needed.

Rules:

- Make tracking IDs configurable, not hardcoded.
- Respect cookie consent before non-essential tracking.
- Prefer a trusted plugin for CMP/consent if available.
- Protect forms with validation, sanitization, nonce checks, and spam protection.
- Do not store unnecessary personal data in theme code.

## Reusable brand theme rules

Keep the theme reusable by isolating brand differences:

- Brand colors as CSS variables.
- Logos in ACF Options.
- Header/footer content in ACF Options.
- Social links in ACF Options.
- Brand-specific copy in CMS fields.
- Minor brand-specific UI differences via modifier classes or config.

Do not duplicate the entire theme for PHYTO and LIERAC.

Do not over-engineer brand abstraction if only PHYTO is being implemented in the current task.

## Agent decision rules

When asked to implement a feature:

- If it is product information, implement it as catalog CPT content.
- If it is product purchase behavior, do not implement it unless the user explicitly changes scope.
- If it is front-end customer auth, do not implement it unless the user explicitly changes scope.
- If it exists on `fr.phyto.com` but is commerce-related, remove or neutralize it.
- If it is a reusable UI pattern, implement it as a template part/component.
- If it is editable content, model it with ACF.
- If it is brand styling, use CSS variables/options.
- If it is SEO-critical content, render it server-side.
- If it is JavaScript-only decoration, avoid it unless necessary.

## Quality checklist

Before finishing work, verify:

- No WooCommerce code was introduced.
- No cart, checkout, buy button, payment, shipping, or stock logic exists.
- No front-end login/register/account/wishlist/order features exist.
- Product pages are CPT catalog pages.
- Product listing cards do not show price or add-to-cart.
- Reference-site UI patterns are recreated without commerce behavior.
- ACF fields are guarded and escaped.
- Images use WordPress image functions.
- Templates follow the WordPress hierarchy.
- CSS is plain, scoped, and low-specificity.
- Content remains editable through WordPress admin.
- Tracking/form code is consent-aware and privacy-conscious.
- The theme remains reasonably reusable for LIERAC/future brands.

